% Origin: Kowalski, *Computational Logic and Human Thinking*,
%         Chapter 1 "Logic on the Underground", sections 1.2 (the
%         disambiguated meaning of the second and third sentences of the
%         Emergency Notice) and 1.4 (forward reasoning over the notice).
% Last verification: no open issues flagged (no errors/warnings; all tests pass).
%
% The London Underground Emergency Notice. Section 1.2 disambiguates the
% notice into two conditionals:
%   - the driver stops the train IN a station if alerted and a part of the
%     train is in the station;
%   - the driver stops the train AT THE NEXT station (where help can be given
%     better than between stations) if alerted and no part of the train is in
%     a station.
% Section 1.4 supplies the background that pressing the alarm signal button
% alerts the driver to an emergency. We keep the rules generic (a driver, a
% train, a station, a passenger) and put the concrete actors in scenarios.
%
% Modeling notes:
%  - The notice speaks of "the driver" of "the train"; to tie a concrete driver
%    and train together we add an auxiliary "a driver drives a train" relation
%    and let the alert be about a particular train. This is a faithful
%    reconstruction of the implicit indexing in the book's wording.
%  - The negation in the third sentence MUST be written on its own line after
%    "it is not the case that" (the multiline form); the inline one-line form
%    is mis-parsed by the current grammar.

the target language is: prolog.

the templates are:
*a passenger* on *a train* presses the alarm signal button.
*a driver* drives *a train*.
*a driver* is alerted to an emergency on *a train*.
*a part* of *a train* is in *a station*.
*a driver* stops *a train* in *a station*.
*a driver* stops *a train* at the next station.

the knowledge base underground includes:

% Background (§1.4): a passenger on a train pressing the alarm signal button
% alerts that train's driver to an emergency.
a driver is alerted to an emergency on a train
    if the driver drives the train
    and a passenger on the train presses the alarm signal button.

% §1.2, second sentence: stop the train IN a station if alerted and any part
% of the train is in the station.
a driver stops a train in a station
    if the driver is alerted to an emergency on the train
    and a part of the train is in the station.

% §1.2, third sentence: stop the train AT THE NEXT station if alerted and no
% part of the train is in a station.
a driver stops a train at the next station
    if the driver is alerted to an emergency on the train
    and it is not the case that
        a part of the train is in a station.

scenario in station is:
    bob drives train1.
    alice on train1 presses the alarm signal button.
    front of train1 is in station1.
    stop in station expects answers ["bob stops train1 in station1"].

scenario between stations is:
    bob drives train1.
    alice on train1 presses the alarm signal button.
    stop at next expects answers ["bob stops train1 at the next station"].

query stop in station is:
    which driver stops which train in which station.

query stop at next is:
    which driver stops which train at the next station.
